// two rules that are invisible in a dump and wrong in a debugger.
//
// **a name this compiler minted is artificial, not hidden.** a `foreach` lowers to a `$__it` cursor and
// a method's `$this` is a parameter nobody typed; both are real storage and both are worth looking at
// when the thing being chased is in the library. DIFlagArtificial is what keeps them out of
// `frame variable` until somebody asks, where dropping the declaration would be the debugger not
// knowing the storage exists
//
// **a block gets its own scope**, so the two `$v` below resolve to the one that is actually in scope
// rather than to whichever the walk recorded last
function pick(int32 $n) : int32
{
    int32 $v = 100;

    if ($n > 0) {
        int32 $v = 7;
        return $v + $n;
    }

    return $v;
}

array<int32> $xs = [1, 2, 3];
int32 $total = 0;

foreach ($xs as $x) {
    $total = $total + $x;
}

echo pick(5);
echo $total;
